# Copy to .env and fill in. Every EXPO_PUBLIC_ value is written into the app when it is built or bundled, so changing
# one needs the dev server restarted with `npx expo start --clear`.
# What is a secret does not belong here: this file ends up inside the app. The values below are not secrets.

# Where the backend is, for a build that is not run from Metro. While developing the app finds it on its own. The builds
# for the stores (eas.json: preview, production) take it from the EAS environment of the same name, not from this file,
# and stop without it: docs/release.md.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.com

# Sign in with Google: the OAuth client IDs of your Google Cloud project. Where to get them, step by step:
# docs/google-sign-in.md
#
# The Web client ID. The app asks Google for a token addressed to it, and the backend accepts only tokens addressed to
# the IDs in GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS (backend/.env), so put this one there too.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
# The iOS client ID. The app's URL scheme is made from it when the app is built, so changing it needs a new build
# (`npx expo prebuild --platform ios`, then `npx expo run:ios`), not just a reload.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=

# Sign in with Google and with Apple in the browser (the web build): docs/web-sign-in.md. The web build asks Google for the token
# with the same Web client ID as above, once the address it is opened from is among that client's "Authorized JavaScript
# origins". Apple needs a Services ID of its own, and an https address registered for it: until it is set there is no Apple button
# in the browser. The backend lists it next to the bundle ID, in APPLE_CLIENT_IDS (backend/.env). Not a secret either.
EXPO_PUBLIC_APPLE_SERVICES_ID=
